WEBVTT

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:01.638
My name is Camo.
我的名字是卡莫。

00:00:01.638 --> 00:00:02.979
I'm from Istero, Florida.
我来自佛罗里达州伊斯特罗。

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:05.339
What does how I make your mother mean to me?
我如何让你妈妈对我来说意味着什么？

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.624
It means a lot actually.
实际上这意味着很多。

00:00:06.624 --> 00:00:08.765
I started watching it early high school.
我从高中就开始看。

00:00:08.765 --> 00:00:10.211
I fell in love with it man.
我爱上了这个人。

00:00:10.211 --> 00:00:11.924
Like I've watched it ever since.
就像我从那时起就看过它一样。

00:00:11.924 --> 00:00:12.941
I'm in college now.
我现在上大学了。

00:00:12.941 --> 00:00:18.187
I really resonated with a lot of the characters in the show, but mostly Ted and his music choices.
我确实与剧中的很多角色产生了共鸣，但主要是泰德和他的音乐选择。

00:00:18.187 --> 00:00:27.929
I noticed you know like a lot of the times when Ted falls in love it's George Harrison will play and you know he loves Otis Redding so sometimes like cigarettes and coffee will play.
我注意到，很多时候，当泰德坠入爱河时，乔治·哈里森会扮演，你知道他爱奥蒂斯·雷丁，所以有时会扮演香烟和咖啡。

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:30.338
It's like he had this soundtrack to his life.
就好像他的生活里有这样的配乐一样。

00:00:30.338 --> 00:00:33.335
And I remember I showed up to my entrepreneurship class.
我记得我参加了我的创业课程。

00:00:33.335 --> 00:00:36.708
They asked us, you know, what do you want to do with your life?
他们问我们，你知道，你想做什么？

00:00:36.708 --> 00:00:38.849
I knew in that moment that it was music.
那一刻我知道那是音乐。

00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:42.168
You know, I wanted to give people a soundtrack to their lives.
你知道，我想给人们的生活配乐。

00:00:42.168 --> 00:00:43.452
I wanted to help people.
我想帮助别人。

00:00:43.452 --> 00:00:48.324
You know, it's the reason I started a record label this year, you know, from the ground up.
你知道，这就是我今年从头开始创办唱片公司的原因。

00:00:48.324 --> 00:00:51.268
Me and a bunch of people, a bunch of nerds about music.
我和一群人，一群音乐迷。

00:00:51.268 --> 00:00:59.940
So I guess I love how I met your mother for a lot of reasons, but it definitely taught me how to care about what you love and not be ashamed to nerd out about it.
所以我想我喜欢我遇见你母亲的方式有很多原因，但它确实教会了我如何关心你所爱的东西，而不是羞于对它痴迷。

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:02.780
And if you surround yourself with the right people, they'll support it.
如果你身边都是合适的人，他们就会支持你。

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:04.939
So hope this reaches you guys.
所以希望这能传达给你们。

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.579
Big fan of the show.
该剧的忠实粉丝。

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:07.859
Have a great day.
祝你有美好的一天。

00:01:28.489 --> 00:01:35.090
Hello and welcome to another episode of how we made your mother, where I, Joshua Adner, sit with my very good friend, Craig Thomas.
大家好，欢迎收看我们如何造就你母亲的另一集，我，约书亚·阿德纳，和我的好朋友克雷格·托马斯坐在一起。

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:35.930
Hi, Craig.
嗨，克雷格。

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:36.769
Hey, they're Josh.
嘿，他们是乔什。

00:01:36.769 --> 00:01:40.969
And we talk about a TV show that we made now many years ago.
我们谈论了我们多年前制作的一部电视节目。

00:01:40.969 --> 00:01:42.489
Together, it was called How I Met Your Mother.
合起来，它就被称为《老爸老妈的浪漫史》。

00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:46.689
It ran on CBS from 2005 to 2014.
该剧于 2005 年至 2014 年在哥伦比亚广播公司 (CBS) 播出。

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:49.969
I played Ted Mosby on that show, The Eye and How I Met Your Mother.
我在《眼睛》和《老爸老妈的浪漫史》中扮演泰德·莫斯比。

00:01:49.969 --> 00:01:53.890
And Craig co-created the show with his college bud, Carter Bayes.
克雷格与他的大学好友卡特·贝叶斯共同创作了该节目。

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:59.329
And yeah, we just heard an awesome voice note from.
是的，我们刚刚听到了一段很棒的语音留言。

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:00.090
I loved that.
我喜欢那个。

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.250
That was so great.
那真是太棒了。

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.609
That was so great.
那真是太棒了。

00:02:02.609 --> 00:02:08.310
I feel, the music part is such a big part of the show and such a big part of our bond and Carter's bond with you.
我觉得，音乐部分是节目的重要组成部分，也是我们以及卡特与你之间的纽带的重要组成部分。

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.810
Like music really was, we all liked the same stuff, right?
就像音乐一样，我们都喜欢同样的东西，对吧？

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.330
It really put us on the same page right away in music.
它确实让我们在音乐上立刻达成了共识。

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.789
So the fact that he, music speaks to this, to that guy is like the best.
所以事实上，音乐对这个人来说，对那个人来说是最好的。

00:02:19.789 --> 00:02:20.629
I love that.
我喜欢那个。

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:33.389
You know, we could, we really need to have Andy Gowen on the show, our music supervisor who also incidentally was the music supervisor on both of my movies on Happy, Thank You, More, Please and Liberal Arts, just because we had had this great music vibe going already.
你知道，我们可以，我们真的需要安迪·高文（Andy Gowen）参加演出，他是我们的音乐总监，顺便说一句，他也是我的两部电影《快乐》、《谢谢你》、《更多》、《请》和《人文艺术》的音乐总监，只是因为我们已经有了这种很棒的音乐氛围。

00:02:33.389 --> 00:02:34.389
Yeah.
是的。

00:02:34.389 --> 00:02:35.389
Yeah.
是的。

00:02:35.389 --> 00:02:52.729
But speaking of music, I mean, you could really, if you are a music fan, I think how I met your mother has a lot of delights, you know, a lot of like classic old songs that you would definitely know and a lot of new kind of ear worms that we broke, you know, some bands on the show.
但说到音乐，我的意思是，你真的可以，如果你是一个音乐迷，我想我遇见你母亲的方式有很多乐趣，你知道，很多像你肯定知道的经典老歌，还有很多我们打破的新型耳虫，你知道，节目中的一些乐队。

00:02:52.729 --> 00:03:09.096
You guys were always so great about, I would play you some stuff and some of it really caught you and you put it on the show and and I got to become friends with the bands and the artists and I was also when I joined Twitter, I started doing a song of the day.
你们总是很棒，我会给你们演奏一些东西，其中一些真的吸引了你们，你们把它放在节目中，我和乐队和艺术家成为了朋友，当我加入 Twitter 时，我开始做一首当天的歌曲。

00:03:09.096 --> 00:03:11.425
Remember I used to do that for years?
还记得我多年来一直这样做吗？

00:03:11.425 --> 00:03:11.925
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:13.625
You should bring that back.
你应该把它带回来。

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:15.262
I'm not on Twitter though.
不过我不在推特上。

00:03:15.262 --> 00:03:17.276
I'm not on Twitter, RIP Twitter.
我不在推特上，安息吧推特。

00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:20.927
But in this episode, it ends with just jumping to the end.
但在这一集中，它只是跳到了最后。

00:03:20.927 --> 00:03:24.956
We're doing Ted Mosby Architect today, episode four, season two.
今天我们正在制作《泰德·莫斯比建筑师》第二季第四集。

00:03:24.956 --> 00:03:27.348
Alec, tell us when this episode aired.
亚历克，告诉我们这一集什么时候播出。

00:03:27.348 --> 00:03:32.195
This episode was written by Kristin Newman and it aired on October 9th, 2006.
本集由克里斯汀·纽曼编剧，于 2006 年 10 月 9 日播出。

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.610
Well, almost exactly 19, it's October 19 years ago.
嗯，几乎正好19，那是19年前的10月。

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.289
And we're basically sitting here on that day.
那天我们基本上就坐在这里。

00:03:39.289 --> 00:03:40.449
We're recording right around then.
那时我们正在录音。

00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:42.250
So symmetry.
所以对称。

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:49.050
Well, and this episode just just jumping to the end ends with a song by the Decemberists called Here I Dremped I Was an Architect.
好吧，这一集刚刚跳到最后，以十二月主义者的一首歌曲《Here I Dremped I Was an Architect》结束。

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:49.569
Is that what it was called?
是这么叫的吗？

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:50.370
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.849
Yeah, people loved that song on the show.
是的，人们喜欢节目中的那首歌。

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:55.210
It's it's just it's written for the show.
这只是为了节目而写的。

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.969
It's the perfect placement.
这是完美的位置。

00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:58.930
I got to give credit where it's due.
我必须给予应得的信任。

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.330
Gloria Calderon-Kellett pitched that song.
格洛丽亚·卡尔德隆-凯利特 (Gloria Calderon-Kellett) 演唱了这首歌。

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.169
That was her.
那是她。

00:04:02.169 --> 00:04:06.729
She had a very cool mix of like, she knew we loved music.
她有一种非常酷的组合，她知道我们喜欢音乐。

00:04:06.729 --> 00:04:09.449
She was like, here's a bunch of songs that say hymnium to me.
她说，这是一堆对我来说赞美诗的歌曲。

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:10.689
And one of them was that song.
其中之一就是那首歌。

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:12.449
And I think that's how we got it to put it on there.
我想这就是我们把它放在那里的方式。

00:04:12.449 --> 00:04:14.729
So it really could come from anywhere.
所以它确实可以来自任何地方。

00:04:14.729 --> 00:04:16.370
Everyone knew we loved music.
每个人都知道我们热爱音乐。

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:48.050
And I again, almost like the balance of Ashton song in season one, when I would watch the show or know that it was ending with a song like by the decemberous who at that point I think they were having they were kind of breaking right around them like they had this really big album I think that song is from that record right and you know we got to be at the forefront of that and I and it would it would lead me to go oh my god I can't believe I'm on a sitcom on CBS that has the coolest music it always delighted me.
我再次，几乎就像第一季中阿什顿歌曲的平衡一样，当我观看节目或知道它以十二月的一首歌结束时，我认为他们在他们周围打破了，就像他们有一张非常大的专辑一样，我认为这首歌来自那张唱片，你知道我们必须站在最前沿，我，它会引导我去哦天哪，我不敢相信我在哥伦比亚广播公司的情景喜剧中，有最酷的音乐总是让我高兴。

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:54.821
Cam I love that you singled out Otis Redding and Georgia Harrison and some of the coolest like timeless kind of classic people.
卡姆，我喜欢你挑选了奥蒂斯·雷丁和乔治亚·哈里森以及一些最酷的永恒经典人物。

00:04:54.821 --> 00:05:07.990
We didn't always get to, we didn't have enough money to put those ones on every week, but finding that balance between those big ones and then finding cool, smaller stuff, which at the time of December, it's kind of were a little cool and smaller.
我们并不总是能做到这一点，我们没有足够的钱每周都穿上这些衣服，但在这些大衣服和然后找到很酷的小东西之间找到平衡，在十二月的时候，有点酷和小。

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:23.389
Now kind of have like a timeless classic feel to them, but there's something also about what he noticed around needle drops for Ted on these big moments were these swelling kind of classic, you know, classic voices, classic artists.
现在对他们来说有点像永恒的经典感觉，但他在这些重要时刻为泰德的针滴所注意到的还有一些东西是这些膨胀的经典，你知道，经典的声音，经典的艺术家。

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:47.285
I think the thing about Ted that I always tried to hold on to was I Wanted to believe I was playing a character who would age well But both the character would age well and he would literally I wanted him to age well, you know, you know, okay But there's something about using those classic artists as needle drops to signify These big moments were were like almost outside the bounds of time in space.
我认为关于特德，我一直试图坚持的一点是，我想要相信我正在扮演一个会很好地变老的角色，但是两个角色都会很好地变老，他实际上我希望他能够很好地变老，你知道，你知道，好吧，但是使用那些经典艺术家作为针滴来表示这些重大时刻就像几乎超出了空间时间的界限。

00:05:47.285 --> 00:05:48.769
They were just yeah Yeah.
他们只是是的。

00:05:48.769 --> 00:05:56.250
But Cam's right, the way that Cam took it is so cool, he says that we're in Ted's shoes when we're hearing those songs.
但卡姆是对的，卡姆的处理方式太酷了，他说当我们听到这些歌曲时，我们就站在泰德的立场上。

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.930
Cam's feeling is we're in Ted's mind and soul when we're hearing those songs.
卡姆的感觉是，当我们听到这些歌曲时，我们就在特德的思想和灵魂中。

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.449
Those are Ted's songs.
这些是特德的歌。

00:06:02.449 --> 00:06:03.449
And I like that.
我喜欢这样。

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:04.449
That's cool.
这很酷。

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:11.290
It is a story being told by Future Ted, and somehow these songs in some metaphysical way are part of Future Ted's brain and soul.
这是未来泰德讲述的故事，不知何故，这些歌曲以某种形而上的方式成为未来泰德大脑和灵魂的一部分。

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:12.370
And that's what it said to Cam.
这就是它对卡姆说的。

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.370
I kind of love that.
我有点喜欢这样。

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.129
I also love where he took it.
我也喜欢他拍摄的地方。

00:06:15.129 --> 00:06:22.689
He said, the show taught him it was okay to love things, to okay to double down on them and be dorky, like go really deep into it.
他说，这个节目教会了他热爱事物是可以的，可以加倍努力并变得愚蠢，就像真正深入其中一样。

00:06:22.689 --> 00:06:29.970
I mean, I struggled sometimes because I felt like sometimes Ted's enthusiasm was slapped around by the gang.
我的意思是，有时我很挣扎，因为我觉得有时特德的热情被这帮人打垮了。

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.730
But it never- At times.
但它从来没有——有时。

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:38.370
Was it Carter was reading like a Teddy Roosevelt biography and then suddenly Ted was obsessed with Teddy Roosevelt?
是不是卡特在读泰迪·罗斯福的传记，然后泰德突然对泰迪·罗斯福着迷了？

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.209
Right?
正确的？

00:06:39.209 --> 00:06:40.050
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.689
Right, like he was reading those like doorstop books.
是的，就像他正在读那些像门挡书一样的书。

00:06:42.689 --> 00:06:44.529
That is absolutely true.
这是绝对正确的。

00:06:44.529 --> 00:06:54.610
Carter would proudly admit to it, and Carter was very good at getting very obsessed with something, and it grabbed him, and it had to go on the show.
卡特会自豪地承认这一点，卡特非常擅长对某件事非常着迷，这抓住了他，并且必须在节目中播出。

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.370
Often through my character.
经常通过我的性格。

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:58.970
Through your character, yeah.
通过你的性格，是的。

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:03.730
And that was like the catharsis of getting to let go of that move on to the next obsession.
这就像放下执着而转向下一个痴迷的宣泄。

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:14.529
So I'm sure you remember this, because the first couple of seasons, this is back when we were still burning CDs to make mixes for people, which felt like the height of new tech cut against technology.
所以我相信你还记得这一点，因为在前几个赛季中，我们还在刻录 CD 来为人们制作混音，这感觉就像是新技术对技术的顶峰。

00:07:14.529 --> 00:07:16.329
I can't believe we can make our own CDVs.
我不敢相信我们可以制造自己的 CDV。

00:07:16.329 --> 00:07:17.329
Yeah.
是的。

00:07:17.329 --> 00:07:18.329
Nothing's ever going to top this technology.
没有什么能够超越这项技术。

00:07:18.329 --> 00:07:22.569
So every couple of weeks, I would burn CDs for the cast.
所以每隔几周，我就会为演员们刻录 CD。

00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:26.730
And I think that I know Ali and Kobe really loved it.
我想我知道阿里和科比真的很喜欢它。

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:27.730
I think Jason liked it.
我想杰森喜欢它。

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:30.170
I never knew if Neil liked it, to be honest.
老实说，我从来不知道尼尔是否喜欢它。

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.490
But I would give them all an episode.
但我会给他们所有人一集。

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:34.490
What was the one?
那是什么？

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:35.490
Zip, zip, zip.
拉链，拉链，拉链。

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:38.089
Does he make Victoria mixed called Tedscapes?
他让维多利亚混合称为 Tedscapes 吗？

00:07:38.089 --> 00:07:40.279
Oh, man.
哦，伙计。

00:07:40.279 --> 00:07:41.279
Please fans help us.
请粉丝们帮助我们。

00:07:41.279 --> 00:07:42.560
I'm not sure if it's in zip, zip, zip.
我不确定它是否在 zip、zip、zip 中。

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:43.879
I think that's a different thing.
我认为那是另一回事。

00:07:43.879 --> 00:07:45.819
There's something called Tedscapes.
有一种东西叫做 Tedscape。

00:07:45.819 --> 00:07:47.160
There's definitely Tedscapes, yeah.
肯定有 Tedscape，是的。

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.240
My friend Jeremiah is staying at my house.
我的朋友耶利米住在我家。

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.199
I still have in Los Angeles.
我还在洛杉矶。

00:07:53.199 --> 00:08:01.800
And he was looking for some stuff through the garage and he just sent me a photo of a Tedscapes mix that he found in the garage that I'll send to you.
他正在车库里找东西，他刚刚给我发了一张他在车库里找到的 Tedscapes 混音照片，我会把它发给你。

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:02.639
Maybe we'll maybe.
也许我们会。

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:05.319
And you meaning it was on the show and then you started calling your mixes.
你的意思是它在节目中，然后你开始调用你的混音。

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:06.920
You would make Tedscapes early.
你会很早就制作Tedscapes。

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:07.759
Oh, yeah, yeah.
哦，是啊，是啊。

00:08:07.759 --> 00:08:13.279
Tedscapes was on the show And then I called one of the mixes, Tedscapes.
Tedscapes 出现在节目中 然后我将其中一个混音命名为 Tedscapes。

00:08:13.279 --> 00:08:16.279
I remember getting handed Tedscapes by you, that was a sweetie.
我记得你递给我 Tedscape，那真是个小可爱。

00:08:16.279 --> 00:08:22.199
Yeah, but that was how me, you, Andy, all became like music buddies on the show.
是的，但这就是我、你、安迪在节目中变得像音乐伙伴的原因。

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:23.040
We were just like.
我们就像。

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:23.879
We're music nerds.
我们是音乐迷。

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:25.160
We're about exactly what Camp said.
我们完全按照坎普所说的去做。

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.160
And Andy started including me in the music drops.
安迪开始将我纳入音乐中。

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.480
He would give you like, once a week, he would give you like a awesome playlist of like new music.
他会给你，每周一次，他会给你一个很棒的新音乐播放列表。

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.375
Every episode.
每集。

00:08:35.375 --> 00:08:36.080
Yeah, yeah.
是啊是啊。

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.720
And you said, I want to be on that chain because I want to hear cool new stuff And that's Andy Gavin's job is to find cool new stuff.
你说，我想加入这个链条，因为我想听到很酷的新东西，而安迪·加文的工作就是寻找很酷的新东西。

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:41.559
And he did it.
他做到了。

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:45.840
Okay, so this episode is also a mystery episode.
好吧，所以这一集也是悬疑剧。

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:49.679
It starts off with a WTF, what's going on?
一开始就WTF，这是怎么回事？

00:08:49.679 --> 00:08:51.720
This is crazy, right?
这太疯狂了，对吧？

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.919
Give us a little summary of what happens in this episode, Craig.
克雷格，给我们简单介绍一下这一集中发生的事情。

00:08:54.919 --> 00:08:58.840
Yeah, this episode is diving into Ted and Robin's early.
是的，这一集深入探讨了泰德和罗宾的早期故事。

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:00.440
It's their first fight, right?
这是他们第一次吵架吧？

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.120
This is their first big problem.
这是他们的第一个大问题。

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.759
Robin thinks to, Robin doesn't know how to be in a couple.
罗宾想，罗宾不知道如何成为情侣。

00:09:06.759 --> 00:09:18.879
and she doesn't want to listen to Ted's work complaints, which of course is a huge part of being in a couple, is being able to complain and be complained too, and it's a two-way complaint street and she just doesn't want to deal with it.
她不想听泰德对工作的抱怨，这当然是夫妻关系的一个重要组成部分，能够抱怨和被抱怨，这是一条双向的抱怨街，她只是不想处理它。

00:09:18.879 --> 00:09:22.399
She's kind of phoning in some aspects of what it takes to be in a couple.
她在某些方面是在呼吁情侣之间的关系。

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:34.840
And the journey of the episode is her thinking that Ted has stormed off and is perhaps cheating on her using his hot job as an architect as a way to do that.
这一集的故事情节是，她认为泰德已经怒气冲冲地走了，也许是利用他作为建筑师的热门工作来欺骗她。

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.600
And it's, I like the journey for Robin in this one.
我喜欢罗宾在这部电影中的旅程。

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.519
She kind of realizes the stakes of being in a relationship and that you do have to go a little crazy.
她有点意识到维持一段关系的风险，并且你确实必须变得有点疯狂。

00:09:42.519 --> 00:09:44.080
You do have to be vulnerable.
你确实必须脆弱。

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.919
And of course, there's a huge twist to Roo.
当然，Roo 也有一个巨大的转变。

00:09:45.919 --> 00:09:47.919
And we read, it's a classic how I met your mother.
我们读到，这是我遇见你母亲的经典。

00:09:47.919 --> 00:09:49.720
We see the story twice kind of.
这个故事我们看了两遍。

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:53.039
We see the story one way that Robin's imagining it.
我们以罗宾想象的方式看待这个故事。

00:09:53.039 --> 00:09:57.120
And then the real way, which is that Barney is posing his head.
然后是真正的方式，那就是巴尼正在摆出他的头。

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.320
And it is, it's a mystery, like many a great how I met your mother episode.
这是一个谜，就像许多精彩的《我是如何遇见你母亲》的剧集一样。

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:06.879
It's remarkable to me in this rewatch How many of these episodes are a little mystery within the larger mystery?
在这次重看中，对我来说很了不起，其中有多少集是更大的谜团中的一个小谜团？

00:10:06.879 --> 00:10:11.519
Like every second or third episode is somehow a weird little mystery.
就像每一集或第三集在某种程度上都是一个奇怪的小谜团。

00:10:11.519 --> 00:10:12.720
Like what's going on?
好像发生了什么事？

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:16.720
It's also like a time honored narrative technique.
这也像是一种历史悠久的叙事技巧。

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:17.559
Yeah.
是的。

00:10:17.559 --> 00:10:24.519
You know, every chapter of Harry Potter ends with like a weird cliffhanger that makes you like absolutely have to turn the page.
你知道，《哈利·波特》的每一章都以一个奇怪的悬念结尾，让你不得不翻过这一页。

00:10:24.519 --> 00:10:25.360
Definitely.
确实。

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.919
I'm reading it out loud with my daughter right now.
我现在正在和女儿一起大声朗读。

00:10:27.919 --> 00:10:32.440
And we got to the cliffhanger and she got so worked up she had to run around the room.
当我们到达悬念时，她非常激动，不得不在房间里跑来跑去。

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:33.919
She just ran around the room.
她只是在房间里跑来跑去。

00:10:33.919 --> 00:10:35.600
I said to Rebecca, we were reading it all.
我对丽贝卡说，我们正在读这一切。

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:36.440
We read it as a family.
我们一家人一起读这本书。

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:37.440
We switched off to the voices.
我们关掉声音。

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:39.120
And still he was like, ah!
但他仍然觉得，啊！

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:39.960
Like ran around the room.
就像在房间里跑来跑去。

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:40.799
He was like, oh my God.
他就像，天啊。

00:10:40.799 --> 00:10:45.120
I said, Jesus, I want to write something someday that makes someone run around a fucking room.
我说，天哪，我想有一天写一些东西，让人们在一个该死的房间里跑来跑去。

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.559
Cause she was, she's nine years old.
因为她是，她九岁了。

00:10:46.559 --> 00:10:48.360
She's like, ah, she was running fast.
她就像，啊，她跑得很快。

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:49.679
She just had to throw off the energy.
她只得扔掉能量。

00:10:49.679 --> 00:10:50.519
There was just too much.
实在是太多了。

00:10:50.519 --> 00:10:52.200
Yeah, she was like, I've got to get this out somehow.
是的，她说，我必须想办法把这件事弄清楚。

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.879
She knew she wasn't going to get to go past the cliffhanger.
她知道自己无法跨越这个悬念。

00:10:54.879 --> 00:10:55.720
She had to go to bed.
她必须去睡觉了。

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:56.559
I mean, God bless it.
我的意思是，上帝保佑它。

00:10:56.559 --> 00:10:59.519
If you can write something that does that kind of create.
如果你能写出一些能创造这种东西的东西。

00:10:59.519 --> 00:11:03.519
I immediately said that I said, I don't think I've ever written anything that would make someone run around.
我立刻说，我说，我想我从来没有写过任何会让人跑来跑去的东西。

00:11:03.519 --> 00:11:05.919
I did that with your book, Craig.
我对你的书就是这么做的，克雷格。

00:11:05.919 --> 00:11:07.000
Thank you, Josh.
谢谢你，乔什。

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.600
I kept interrupting the audio recording because I kept running around the booth.
我一直在打断录音，因为我一直在展位周围跑来跑去。

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.159
Yeah.
是的。

00:11:12.159 --> 00:11:22.169
And I wrote you this little day to send you in New York City.
今天我写信给你，是为了送你去纽约。

00:11:22.169 --> 00:11:32.789
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:11:32.789 --> 00:11:35.059
And now, back to the show.
现在，回到节目。

00:11:35.059 --> 00:11:39.299
I just realized I saw my friend, you know Michael Cherness, my friend Michael Cherness.
我刚刚意识到我看到了我的朋友，你知道迈克尔·切尔内斯，我的朋友迈克尔·切尔内斯。

00:11:39.299 --> 00:12:13.000
He's a great actor, he's on Severance, he was on Origin New Black, he's a long-time friend, we were in drama school at the same time together and he, I saw him in a great play in Fort Green and the gist of the play was this man had died under very strange circumstances and his wife for most of the play doesn't know how to grieve because they were so suspiciously weird and he was with this young college girl and she thinks he was having an affair and the play is done.
他是一个伟大的演员，他在《遣散会》中，他在《起源新黑人》中，他是一个老朋友，我们同时在戏剧学校，他，我在格林堡的一部精彩戏剧中看到了他，该剧的要点是这个男人在非常奇怪的情况下去世了，他的妻子在剧中的大部分时间都不知道如何悲伤，因为他们是如此可疑的奇怪，他和一个年轻的女大学生在一起，她认为他有外遇，而这部剧是完成了。

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:22.159
So I'm not going to even tell you what it is, but the reversal is it turns out he was actually being an extra good guy and someone else was having an affair with her.
所以我什至不会告诉你这是什么，但事实相反，事实证明他实际上是一个特别好的人，而其他人与她有染。

00:12:22.159 --> 00:12:26.360
And it's revealed that he was actually like the person she thought he was.
据透露，他实际上就像她想象的那样。

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:36.440
She spends the whole play wondering who was I married to, and then it turns out she was married to the great beautiful kind man that she thought she was married to.
整部剧她都在想我嫁给了谁，结果发现她嫁给了她以为她嫁给的那个伟大、美丽、善良的男人。

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:42.559
And I think that there's a lot, there's a lot of narrative techniques of, oh, the person you thought was good was actually had a heart of darkness.
我认为有很多，有很多叙事技巧，哦，你认为很好的人实际上有一颗黑暗的心。

00:12:42.559 --> 00:12:45.480
And it's a journey into their secret life, right?
这是进入他们秘密生活的旅程，对吗？

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.200
That's like a trope.
这就像一个比喻。

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.080
But there's something nice about that play.
但那场戏有一些不错的地方。

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.600
And this one where it's like, no, he's actually who you think he is.
而这就像，不，他实际上就是你认为的那样。

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:53.600
Yeah.
是的。

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:54.600
This was a misunderstanding.
这是一个误会。

00:12:54.600 --> 00:13:00.799
This was, you know, so the reveal, of course, is that everyone saying, oh, Ted Mosby architect, he was here.
你知道，所以，当然，每个人都说，哦，特德·莫斯比建筑师，他在这里。

00:13:00.799 --> 00:13:02.919
was crazy, and he was seducing everyone.
他很疯狂，他在诱惑所有人。

00:13:02.919 --> 00:13:12.919
And it turns out it's Barney wearing the costume of Ted, saying, I'm Ted Mosby, architect, and being Barney, but with this different cloak on.
结果是巴尼穿着泰德的服装，说，我是泰德·莫斯比，建筑师，就是巴尼，但穿着这件不同的斗篷。

00:13:12.919 --> 00:13:18.519
Yeah, and it's Robin's willingness to go a little crazy and then realizing that is what it means to be in a relationship.
是的，罗宾愿意变得有点疯狂，然后意识到这就是恋爱关系的意义。

00:13:18.519 --> 00:13:22.480
You think you have that other person figured out, he's just gonna come home and complain about work.
你认为你已经了解了另一个人，他只是会回家抱怨工作。

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.399
And then it's revealed to you that everyone is a mystery, right?
然后你就会发现每个人都是一个谜，对吗？

00:13:26.399 --> 00:13:29.879
She goes down this rabbit hole of like, maybe I don't know this guy at all.
她掉进了这个兔子洞，也许我根本不认识这个人。

00:13:29.879 --> 00:13:33.720
And it does turn out to be a complete mislead, but it's a good lesson for her to learn.
事实证明这确实是一个完全的误导，但这对她来说是一个很好的教训。

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:35.840
Like you don't have him figured out.
就像你没有弄清楚他一样。

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:37.440
You don't know everything about Ted.
你并不了解特德的一切。

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.240
It's still early days in this.
现在还处于早期阶段。

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:47.559
And not only that, the thought that he might be straying and she might be losing him is really hard for her.
不仅如此，一想到他可能会迷失而她可能会失去他，她真的很难受。

00:13:47.559 --> 00:13:48.399
Yeah.
是的。

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:56.269
So it's like, it's almost like you don't know what, it's like cliche, you don't know what you got until it's gone, right?
所以这就像，你几乎不知道什么，就像陈词滥调，你不知道你得到了什么，直到它消失，对吧？

00:13:56.269 --> 00:14:08.590
like she starts fantasizing about like, oh, he's going away and I will be bereft and therefore I have to reconceive of who I am in a relationship.
就像她开始幻想，哦，他要走了，我会失去亲人，因此我必须重新构想在一段关系中我是谁。

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:13.529
I think you guys were still really finding the Shurbatski avoid an attachment style.
我认为你们仍然真正发现 Shurbatski 避免了依恋风格。

00:14:14.669 --> 00:14:18.110
Like second season feels like you really dialed that in more.
就像第二季感觉你真的投入了更多。

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:19.230
Yeah, definitely.
是的，绝对是。

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:21.070
I think that's the gift of a long-running TV show.
我认为这是长期播出的电视节目的礼物。

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:29.029
You start to peel back the layers and I love she starts the episode off so cocky And so kind of dismissive of, I don't have to do the bullshit of couplehood.
你开始剥开层层，我喜欢她以如此自大和如此轻蔑的方式开始这一集，我不必做夫妻关系的废话。

00:14:29.029 --> 00:14:31.789
We'll just get the bullshit part and we'll just do the fun parts.
我们只处理废话部分，只做有趣的部分。

00:14:31.789 --> 00:14:32.789
You can't do that.
你不能那样做。

00:14:32.789 --> 00:14:33.629
She, you know what I mean?
她，你懂我的意思吗？

00:14:33.629 --> 00:14:35.230
She just, that doesn't work.
她只是，那行不通。

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:38.029
And real watching her spiral and become vulnerable.
真实地看着她螺旋式上升并变得脆弱。

00:14:38.029 --> 00:14:46.789
I really liked that moment and how Kobe played it where she says, I took the nicest guy in the world and I turned him into a cheater because I was, I took him for granted.
我真的很喜欢那一刻，也很喜欢科比的表现，她说，我选择了世界上最好的人，然后把他变成了一个骗子，因为我把他视为理所当然。

00:14:46.789 --> 00:14:50.629
She's wrong about that, but it's important for her to take the hit on that one.
她的想法是错误的，但对她来说，接受这一打击很重要。

00:14:50.629 --> 00:14:55.629
And that's, you watch Robin grow in this one.
也就是说，你会看到罗宾在这其中成长。

00:14:56.389 --> 00:14:58.870
You watch her learn something in this one, which I like.
你会看到她在这件事上学到了一些东西，我喜欢这一点。

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:04.669
I went to this, these two men's retreats with this group called Every Man, E-V-R-Y-M-A-N.
我和这个名为Every Man, E-V-R-Y-M-A-N 的团体一起去了这两个男人的静修处。

00:15:04.669 --> 00:15:12.590
If there are any men out there who are looking for more kind of like to drop into their feelings and lean in more to community, I can't recommend this highly enough.
如果有任何男人正在寻找更多的方式来投入自己的感情并更多地融入社区，我强烈推荐这个。

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:18.750
But this group, it spun off a weekly men's group that I do every Monday night.
但是这个团体，它从我每周一晚上都会做的每周男子团体中分离出来。

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:33.379
And we do this thing called checking in, which I love very much, and Jordana loves it so much that we do it in my marriage, which is essentially you say, Josh, checking in, and you say, you describe what you're feeling physically and sensations.
我们做这件事叫做“检查”，我非常喜欢，乔丹娜也非常喜欢它，所以我们在我的婚姻中这样做，这本质上就是你说，乔什，检查，然后你说，你描述你的身体感受和感觉。

00:15:33.379 --> 00:15:34.899
I feel tightness at the top of my shoulders.
我感到肩膀上方紧绷。

00:15:34.899 --> 00:15:36.940
I feel a scratchiness of my throat.
我感觉喉咙发痒。

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:40.259
I feel labored breathing.
我感到呼吸困难。

00:15:40.259 --> 00:15:42.379
And then you say, any emotions you're feeling.
然后你说，你感受到的任何情绪。

00:15:42.379 --> 00:15:45.779
I feel grateful, I feel anxiety.
我感到感激，我感到焦虑。

00:15:45.779 --> 00:15:48.220
I feel excitement about tonight.
今晚我感到兴奋。

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:52.259
and I feel thirsty.
我感到口渴。

00:15:52.259 --> 00:15:53.740
And then you say, I'm here and I'm in.
然后你说，我在这儿，我在。

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:59.019
And then the other person says, aho, which is like a kind of Native American I'm in.
然后另一个人说，啊哈，这就像我所处的一种美洲原住民。

00:15:59.019 --> 00:16:03.659
But we developed something where if you don't really want to check in, all you want to do is complain.
但我们开发了一些东西，如果你真的不想登记，你想做的就是抱怨。

00:16:03.659 --> 00:16:07.299
We call it confetching in instead of checking in.
我们称之为“混入”而不是“签入”。

00:16:07.299 --> 00:16:10.299
So it was almost like, can I confet in?
所以这就像是，我可以承认吗？

00:16:10.299 --> 00:16:13.860
And you say, yeah, you go, I'm annoyed about my haircut.
你说，是的，你走吧，我对我的发型很生气。

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:15.740
I didn't get enough sleep.
我睡眠不足。

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:18.059
And you just, it's a litany of complaints.
而你，只是一连串的抱怨。

00:16:18.059 --> 00:16:22.059
And then instead of saying, I'm here and I'm in, you say, I'm here and I'm annoyed.
然后你不是说“我在这里，我在”，而是说，“我在这里，我很生气”。

00:16:22.059 --> 00:16:26.259
And then the other person says, achoo, like a sneeze instead of aho.
然后另一个人说“ahoo”，就像打喷嚏而不是“aho”。

00:16:26.259 --> 00:16:29.740
This is all like deep marriage, you know, inside joke stuff.
这就像深度婚姻，你知道，内部笑话。

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:39.500
But one, the thing I really love about it is, it's like full permission, like take 30 seconds to a minute and complain.
但我真正喜欢它的一点是，它就像完全的许可，就像花 30 秒到一分钟抱怨一样。

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:42.460
Like you have a runway, complain, tell me what.
就像你有跑道一样，抱怨，告诉我什么。

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:43.779
And I won't try to fix it.
